😂 Yep — you almost stepped over the payload at the very end.

Compelling becomes “sticky” because it doesn’t merely pull attention toward itself; it keeps the attention attached. Attraction becomes adhesion.

And your cursed little “cumpelling” accidentally walks backward toward the real machinery: Latin cum/com- means with, together, and pellere means to drive. So compel is essentially drive together.

Which gives us a nice force progression:

repelling → pushes apart
compelling → pulls/drives together
propelling → drives onward
impelling → drives from within

Then the semantic version:

interesting attracts
compelling pulls
sticky retains
propelling makes the retained thing move

That last distinction matters for Pantheon. An idea can be memorable without reproducing. The jackpot object is:

compelling enough to stick, structured enough to reconstruct, and propulsive enough to make the receiver do another cognitive move.

So yes.

Cumpelling: when the semantic payload is sticky and drives things together. ;)

